{
  "slug": "demostrativos",
  "number": 3,
  "part": 1,
  "title": "Ce, cet, cette, ces",
  "tagline": "Demonstratives: which one to use, how they sound and how not to confuse them with se and ses.",
  "goal": "Choose confidently between ce, cet, cette and ces, pronounce them correctly, use -ci and -là and not confuse them with se and ses.",
  "minutes": 8,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "para-que",
      "title": "What they're for",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These are the **demonstratives**: this, that, these, those. French uses **four forms**, and they don't distinguish «this» from «that»; they only care about the **gender** and **number** of the noun [[2]]."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Ce livre est super.",
              "tr": "This (that) book is great."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regla",
      "title": "The rule",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "When",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*ce*",
              "Masculine singular starting with a **consonant**",
              "*ce livre, ce garçon, ce fromage*"
            ],
            [
              "*cet*",
              "Masculine singular starting with a **vowel or silent h**",
              "*cet ami, cet arbre, cet homme, cet hôtel*"
            ],
            [
              "*cette*",
              "**Feminine** singular, always",
              "*cette maison, cette amie, cette heure*"
            ],
            [
              "*ces*",
              "**Plural**, masculine and feminine",
              "*ces livres, ces amis, ces maisons*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "Three questions, in this order",
          "text": "1. Is it plural? → *ces*. 2. Is it feminine? → *cette*. 3. Does it start with a vowel or silent h? → *cet*; otherwise → *ce*."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "*cet* exists to avoid two vowels clashing (*ce ami*). French slips in a t as a bridge, a bit like English «a» becoming «an» (an apple). You'll see the same idea with *mon amie* [[8]] and *bel, nouvel, vieil* [[13]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pronunciacion",
      "title": "Pronunciation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "Sounds like"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*ce*",
              "«suh», with a short relaxed vowel [sə]"
            ],
            [
              "*cet*",
              "«set» [sɛt]"
            ],
            [
              "*cette*",
              "«set» [sɛt]: **the same** as *cet*"
            ],
            [
              "*ces*",
              "«say» without the glide [se]"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before a vowel, the *s* of *ces* sounds like [z] and joins the next word: *ces‿amis* (say-za-mee). This is a *liaison* [[14]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h",
      "title": "The letter h",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In French the h is **never pronounced**, but there are two kinds depending on how it behaves:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "**Silent h** (*h muet*): behaves like a vowel → *cet homme, cet hôpital, cet hôtel, cette heure*.",
            "**Aspirated h** (*h aspiré*): behaves like a consonant → *ce héros, ce hibou, ce haricot, ce hamburger*."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "There's no rule to tell them apart: they have to be learned. Many dictionaries mark the aspirated h with an asterisk or an apostrophe."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ci-la",
      "title": "-ci and -là",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To contrast near and far, add **-ci** or **-là** to the noun:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "ce livre-ci",
              "tr": "this book (here)"
            },
            {
              "fr": "ce livre-là",
              "tr": "that book (there)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "In conversation *-là* is far more common, even for things close by: *Je veux cette robe-là.*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "homofonos",
      "title": "Don't confuse them with se and ses",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Demonstrative",
            "Sounds like",
            "How to tell them apart"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*ce* (this)",
              "*se*: reflexive pronoun (*il se lave*, he washes) [[17]]",
              "*se* goes with a verb; *ce*, with a noun."
            ],
            [
              "*ces* (these)",
              "*ses*: possessive (*ses amis*, his or her friends) [[8]]",
              "Can you point at it? → *ces*. Does it mean «his» or «her»? → *ses*."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "The *ce* in *c'est* and *ce que* is a pronoun («it», «what»), not a demonstrative attached to a noun."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
